1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,440
In November 1942, Allied commanders hoped to take Tunis within weeks.

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:12,480
It took six months, and the fighting crossed Tunisia, from its southern desert to its northern hills.

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:19,220
Soldiers came from five continents, and they fought among the towns, farms and villages of the Tunisians.

4
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:27,370
This is the story of the Tunisian campaign, from November 1942 to May 1943.

5
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:42,080
In 1942, Tunisia was a French protectorate. Moncef Bey reigned, but a French resident-general held the real power.

6
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:50,150
He was Admiral Jean-Pierre Esteva, appointed by Vichy France, which had signed an armistice with Germany in 1940.

7
00:00:50,230 --> 00:01:01,000
On 8 November, in Operation Torch, about 125,000 American and British soldiers, sailors and airmen landed in Morocco and Algeria.

8
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:07,820
Within three days, the French there stopped fighting. The real prize lay east, in Tunisia, facing Sicily.

9
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:16,590
Germany moved faster. From 9 November, German planes landed at El Aouina, by Tunis, and Esteva did not stop them.

10
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:23,690
By mid-November, about 15,000 German and 9,000 Italian troops held Tunis and Bizerte.

11
00:01:23,770 --> 00:01:31,840
But General Georges Barré's French troops turned against the Germans, and fought them at Medjez el Bab on 19 November.

12
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:39,630
From Algiers, over 600 kilometres away, the British First Army raced east, with American tanks and French troops.

13
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:46,530
On 28 November, they reached Djedeida, just 21 kilometres from Tunis, and got no closer.

14
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:54,850
Axis fighters flew from hard runways by Tunis, Allied planes from distant dirt fields that the winter rain turned to mud.

15
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:03,980
Counter-attacks drove the Allies back to Medjez el Bab, and on 24 December General Eisenhower called a halt.

16
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:15,520
All winter, the front ran along the mountain ridges. The British held the north, the French the centre, the Americans the south.

17
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:23,040
In January, German attacks fell on the French in the centre. Poorly equipped, they resisted hard, but were overrun.

18
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:31,510
French army records count some 73,000 French troops in Tunisia by March, about 50,000 of them North Africans.

19
00:02:31,590 --> 00:02:37,930
Tunisian riflemen fought among them, with makeshift equipment, beside the Americans and the British.

20
00:02:38,010 --> 00:02:44,470
From the east came Britain's Eighth Army, which had driven the Axis armies out of Egypt and across Libya.

21
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:52,640
With it came New Zealanders, Indians, Free French soldiers from Chad and Greeks, and airmen from Australia and South Africa.

22
00:02:52,720 --> 00:03:01,410
Facing them were Germans and Italians: the Fifth Panzer Army in the north, the Italian First Army in the south.

23
00:03:04,610 --> 00:03:12,800
In February, the Axis struck the Americans in the south. It was the US Army's first campaign against the German army.

24
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:21,290
On 14 February, panzers struck at Sidi Bou Zid, where American infantry sat on hills too far apart to help each other.

25
00:03:21,370 --> 00:03:30,560
In two days, the US 1st Armored Division lost 98 tanks and about half its strength, by the historian Martin Blumenson's count.

26
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,800
The Allies fell back about 80 kilometres, to the passes of the western ridge.

27
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:43,070
On 19 and 20 February, German and Italian troops under Erwin Rommel broke through the Kasserine Pass.

28
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:51,860
They were held at Sbiba, Djebel el Hamra and Thala, where massed British and American guns stopped them on 22 February.

29
00:03:51,940 --> 00:04:02,230
The National WWII Museum names the causes: commanders who had not seen the ground, divisions split up, poor coordination and inexperience.

30
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:11,230
Then the Allies changed. Harold Alexander took command of their armies, and George S. Patton of the US II Corps, on 6 March.

31
00:04:11,310 --> 00:04:18,280
On 23 March, at El Guettar, American troops held against a panzer division.

32
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:28,850
In the south, the Italian First Army under General Giovanni Messe, with German divisions, held the Mareth Line.

33
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:36,950
France had built it in the 1930s against an attack from Italian Libya. Now it faced the British, coming from Libya.

34
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:47,320
On 6 March at Medenine, Rommel attacked and lost 44 to 56 tanks, by the British count. Three days later he left Africa.

35
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:55,490
On the night of 20 March, Montgomery's frontal attack across the Wadi Zigzaou, near the village of Zarat, was thrown back.

36
00:04:55,570 --> 00:05:03,730
Meanwhile the New Zealand Corps, with General Leclerc's Free French, went some 200 kilometres round the hills, to the Tebaga Gap.

37
00:05:03,810 --> 00:05:12,650
This left hook became Montgomery's main attack. On 26 March at 4 p.m., the sun behind it, it broke through.

38
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:21,730
The British official history judged that the hook "won the battle but could not make a victory of it": the Axis got away.

39
00:05:25,130 --> 00:05:31,870
The Axis stood next at the Wadi Akarit, north of Gabès, in a gap between the sea and salt marshes.

40
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:40,390
On the night of 5 April, Indian and Gurkha soldiers climbed its hills in silence, with Scottish and English divisions beside them.

41
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:48,760
Italian and German troops resisted hard, but that night they withdrew. On 7 April, the Eighth Army met the Americans.

42
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:55,800
It pushed north through Sfax and Sousse, more than 200 kilometres, and the two Allied fronts became one.

43
00:05:55,880 --> 00:06:04,770
On 19 and 20 April, the Eighth Army took Enfidaville and the rock village of Takrouna, from Italian and German defenders.

44
00:06:04,850 --> 00:06:14,670
In the west, the British took Longstop Hill and the Americans Hill 609, by 1 May, at a heavy cost on both sides.

45
00:06:17,870 --> 00:06:25,460
On 6 May, the First Army broke through west of Tunis, with Indian infantry and tanks brought from the Eighth Army.

46
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:31,450
On 7 May, British tanks entered Tunis, and American infantry entered Bizerte.

47
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:38,900
The Axis armies were cut in two. British tanks sealed off Cap Bon, and French troops closed in round Zaghouan.

48
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:46,170
Behind them lay the sea, watched day and night by Allied ships and aircraft. Only a few hundred men escaped.

49
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:54,210
On 13 May 1943, the Italian Marshal Giovanni Messe surrendered the last Axis army in Africa.

50
00:06:54,290 --> 00:07:07,030
How many prisoners? The British official history counts 238,243, the US Army's official historian about 275,000.

51
00:07:07,110 --> 00:07:14,220
Historians usually say about a quarter of a million. In Germany, the defeat was compared with Stalingrad.

52
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:25,690
The Allies lost 70,000 to 76,000 men killed, wounded or missing, by American and British counts, and over 11,000 were killed.

53
00:07:25,770 --> 00:07:38,060
German and Italian losses are less certain. The historian Rick Atkinson estimates about 8,500 German and 3,700 Italian dead.

54
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:49,570
And the Tunisians? The historian Mark Willis writes that civilians, caught between the two sides, suffered tremendous losses.

55
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:56,810
Almost all of Tunisia's major cities and towns were destroyed or badly damaged, and its economy was wrecked.

56
00:07:56,890 --> 00:08:04,800
Under the occupation, the Germans had forced about 5,000 Jewish men in Tunisia into labour camps, many near the front.

57
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:14,520
The end of the fighting did not free Tunisia. On 14 May, the French deposed Moncef Bey, accusing him of collaboration.

58
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:24,640
For the Allies, it was a beginning. On 10 July 1943 they landed in Sicily, some sailing from Tunisia, and soon Mussolini fell.

59
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:35,890
The US Army drew lessons: keep units close enough to help each other, keep tanks together, and make every arm work as one.

60
00:08:35,970 --> 00:08:43,730
It left Tunisia with thousands of seasoned soldiers, and commanders such as Patton and Bradley led them in Sicily.

61
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:52,180
Six months of war crossed Tunisia. Remember the soldiers of every army, and the Tunisians whose country it was.
